import{_ as e,c as t,o as a,a4 as o}from"./chunks/framework.ttppV29E.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Endpoints","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/getting-started/endpoints.md","filePath":"docs/getting-started/endpoints.md","lastUpdated":1791474926000}'),n={name:"docs/getting-started/endpoints.md"},s=o('<h1 id="endpoints" tabindex="-1">Endpoints <a class="header-anchor" href="#endpoints" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Endpoints&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Endpoints define and shape the functionality that AireGlu provides. They are called by external applications or other endpoints, and perform various tasks as configured. These can range from manipulating and shaping data, or sending an email, to generating a PDF, to contacting other external services. Custom tasks can also be created via the use of <a href="/docs/advanced/adapters.html">adapters</a>.</p><h2 id="building-an-endpoint" tabindex="-1">Building an Endpoint <a class="header-anchor" href="#building-an-endpoint" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Building an Endpoint&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="./">Getting Started</a> page has a quick setup for a simple endpoint. You should check there for something simple. This guide will go more in depth about the various options available during creation.</p><p><code>Endpoint Name</code> is required, and forms part of the URL for the endpoint. If <code>glu.aireinnovate.com</code> is our AireGlu instance, and <code>airelogic</code> is our tenant, then our full endpoint URL would be <code>https://glu-api.aireinnovate.com/airelogic/ENDPOINT-NAME</code>. The name only allows letters, numbers and hyphens (<code>-</code>) to be used.</p><p><code>Group Name</code> is an optional field and does not affect how the endpoint runs. You can choose to add groups for your endpoints, and then on the endpoint page choose to see your endpoints within their groups. Any endpoints without a group will be shown in the <code>Ungrouped Endpoints</code> group</p><p><code>Error Email</code> is optional. It is used to report the following during endpoint execution:</p><ul><li>There was an input validation error</li><li>Running endpoint resulted in an AireGlu exception</li><li>The endpoint returned a non-success status code</li></ul><h3 id="authentication" tabindex="-1">Authentication <a class="header-anchor" href="#authentication" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Authentication&quot;">​</a></h3><p>You can require authentication for your endpoint to limit availability and use. See <a href="/docs/endpoint-authentication/">Endpoint Authentication</a> for the available methods and how to configure them.</p><h3 id="input" tabindex="-1">Input <a class="header-anchor" href="#input" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Input&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In the input dialog, select the data format the endpoint should expect. You can also edit both the example input and the expected schema (with some exceptions). Learn more <a href="/docs/endpoint-input/input.html">here</a>.</p><h3 id="tasks" tabindex="-1">Tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tasks&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Tasks make up the meat of the Endpoint. They are responsible for the processing that goes on. <a href="/docs/endpoints/tasks.html">Tasks are covered in detail here.</a></p><p><code>Stop on Task Failure</code> allows you to stop running the endpoint when a task fails to complete successfully. If this is turned off, then it will ignore failed tasks, and continue to process each subsequent task. Be aware this may cause unpredictable behaviour or more failures if those tasks depend on the output of the failed task.</p><h3 id="how-the-endpoint-response-is-decided" tabindex="-1">How the endpoint response is decided <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-endpoint-response-is-decided" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the endpoint response is decided&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each task produces its own result and status code, but the endpoint&#39;s HTTP response isn&#39;t simply the last task&#39;s. After the tasks have run, the response is chosen in this order - the first match wins:</p><ol><li><strong><a href="/docs/endpoints/http-redirect.html">HTTP Redirect</a> task</strong> - the caller is redirected.</li><li><strong>Task with <a href="/docs/endpoints/http-request.html#retain-status-code"><code>Retain status code</code></a> enabled</strong> - that task&#39;s status code is returned, with the HTTP Response task&#39;s body and headers if there is one, otherwise that task&#39;s own body.</li><li><strong><a href="/docs/endpoints/http-response.html">HTTP Response</a> task</strong> - its configured status code, body and headers.</li><li><strong>Otherwise:</strong><ul><li>if <code>Stop on Task Failure</code> stopped the endpoint, the failed task&#39;s status code and body.</li><li>if not, <code>200</code> with <a href="/docs/endpoints/http-response.html#omitting-this-task">the results of every task</a>. This includes when tasks failed but <code>Stop on Task Failure</code> is off.</li></ul></li></ol><p>Which tasks count:</p><ul><li>Tasks that were never reached, because <code>Stop on Task Failure</code> stopped the endpoint first, are ignored.</li><li>Tasks skipped by a <a href="/docs/endpoints/tasks.html#filters">filter</a> still count, and return a <code>200</code>. So if a skipped task has <code>Retain status code</code> enabled, the endpoint returns <code>200</code>, even if an earlier task that ran returned something else.</li></ul><p>As <code>Stop on Task Failure</code> is on by default, a failing task&#39;s status code is usually what&#39;s returned - it halts the endpoint before any later HTTP Response task runs.</p><p>Also note:</p><ul><li>An endpoint with no HTTP Request, HTTP Response, HTTP Redirect, SMS, Fax or PDF tasks (e.g. only Mapping or Email tasks) always returns the results of every task, even when <code>Stop on Task Failure</code> stopped it.</li><li>If a task raises an error rather than failing (for example an <a href="/docs/endpoints/http-response-handler.html">HTTP Response Handler</a> schema validation failure), an error response is returned instead.</li></ul>',23),i=[s];function d(r,h,l,p,c,u){return a(),t("div",null,i)}const g=e(n,[["render",d]]);export{m as __pageData,g as default};
